1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:13,500
The uniform code of military justice specifies

2
00:00:13,500 --> 00:00:27,000
court martial for any officer who sends a

3
00:00:27,000 --> 00:00:30,680
soldier into battle without a weapon.

4
00:00:30,680 --> 00:00:32,479
There ought to be a similar protection

5
00:00:32,479 --> 00:00:34,280
for students because students shouldn't go out

6
00:00:34,280 --> 00:00:38,120
into life without an ability to communicate.

7
00:00:38,120 --> 00:00:40,980
And that's because your success in life will

8
00:00:40,980 --> 00:00:43,840
be determined largely by your ability to speak,

9
00:00:43,840 --> 00:00:47,320
your ability to write, and the

10
00:00:47,320 --> 00:00:50,800
quality of your ideas in that order.

11
00:00:50,800 --> 00:00:53,620
I know that I can be successful

12
00:00:53,620 --> 00:00:56,440
in this because the quality of communication.

13
00:00:56,440 --> 00:00:58,800
Your speaking, your writing, is

14
00:00:58,800 --> 00:01:01,160
largely determined by this formula.

15
00:01:01,160 --> 00:01:04,489
It's a matter of how much knowledge you

16
00:01:04,489 --> 00:01:07,820
have, how much you practice with that knowledge,

17
00:01:07,820 --> 00:01:09,780
and your inherent talent.

18
00:01:09,780 --> 00:01:12,400
And notice that the T is very small.

19
00:01:12,400 --> 00:01:14,740
What really matters is what you know.

20
00:01:14,740 --> 00:01:18,320
At this point that came to me suddenly a

21
00:01:18,320 --> 00:01:21,900
few decades ago when I was skiing at Sun Valley.

22
00:01:21,900 --> 00:01:25,300
I had heard that it was Celebrity Weekend.

23
00:01:25,300 --> 00:01:26,360
And one of the celebrities...

24
00:01:26,360 --> 00:01:26,420
Well, he was a famous comedian.

25
00:01:26,440 --> 00:01:31,440
was Mary Lou Retton, famous Olympic gymnast,

26
00:01:33,120 --> 00:01:35,280
perfect tens in the vault.

27
00:01:35,280 --> 00:01:38,460
And I heard that she was a novice at skiing,

28
00:01:38,460 --> 00:01:40,039
so when the opportune moment arrived,

29
00:01:40,039 --> 00:01:42,420
I looked over on the novice slope

30
00:01:42,420 --> 00:01:46,560
and saw this young woman who, when she became unbalanced,

31
00:01:46,560 --> 00:01:48,360
went like that.

32
00:01:48,360 --> 00:01:50,059
And I said, that's got to

33
00:01:50,059 --> 00:01:51,759
be her, that must be the gymnast.

34
00:01:53,560 --> 00:01:55,000
But then it occurred to me,

35
00:01:55,000 --> 00:01:56,860
I'm a much better skier than she is,

36
00:01:56,860 --> 00:01:58,320
and she's an Olympic athlete,

37
00:01:58,320 --> 00:02:02,020
not only an ordinary Olympic athlete, an outstanding one.

38
00:02:03,180 --> 00:02:04,330
And I was a better

39
00:02:04,330 --> 00:02:05,480
skier because I had the K,

40
00:02:06,380 --> 00:02:07,980
and I had the P, and

41
00:02:07,980 --> 00:02:09,580
all she had was the T.

42
00:02:10,440 --> 00:02:12,380
So you can get a lot better than people

43
00:02:12,380 --> 00:02:14,700
who may have inherent talents

44
00:02:14,700 --> 00:02:17,320
if you have the right amount of knowledge.

45
00:02:17,320 --> 00:02:18,960
So that's what my objective is today,

46
00:02:18,960 --> 00:02:20,940
and here's my promise.

47
00:02:20,940 --> 00:02:24,760
Today you will see some examples

48
00:02:24,760 --> 00:02:27,820
of what you can put in your armamentarium

49
00:02:27,820 --> 00:02:29,220
of speaking techniques.

50
00:02:30,440 --> 00:02:32,940
And it will be the case

51
00:02:32,940 --> 00:02:35,440
that some one of those examples,

52
00:02:35,700 --> 00:02:39,299
some heuristic, some technique, maybe only one,

53
00:02:39,299 --> 00:02:41,859
will be the one that gets you the job.

54
00:02:42,899 --> 00:02:45,459
And so this is a very nonlinear process.

55
00:02:45,459 --> 00:02:47,739
You never know when it's gonna happen.

56
00:02:47,739 --> 00:02:48,700
But that is my promise.

57
00:02:48,700 --> 00:02:52,220
By the end of the next 60 minutes,

58
00:02:52,220 --> 00:02:54,760
you'll have been exposed to a lot of ideas,

59
00:02:54,760 --> 00:02:58,100
some of which you'll incorporate into your own repertoire,

60
00:02:58,100 --> 00:03:03,000
and they will ensure that you get the maximum opportunity

61
00:03:03,000 --> 00:03:06,000
to have your ideas valued and accepted

62
00:03:09,340 --> 00:03:11,799
by the people you speak with.

63
00:03:11,799 --> 00:03:13,940
Now, in order to do that,

64
00:03:13,940 --> 00:03:16,700
we have to have a rule of engagement.

65
00:03:16,700 --> 00:03:21,700
And that is no laptops, no cellphones.

66
00:03:22,600 --> 00:03:24,739
So if you could close those, I'll start.

67
00:03:24,760 --> 00:03:27,260
I'll start up as soon as you're done.

68
00:03:27,260 --> 00:03:30,600
Some people ask why that is a rule of engagement.

69
00:03:31,739 --> 00:03:34,980
And the answer is, we humans only have

70
00:03:34,980 --> 00:03:36,959
one language processor.

71
00:03:36,959 --> 00:03:38,780
And if your language processor is engaged,

72
00:03:38,780 --> 00:03:40,540
could you shut the laptop please?

73
00:03:40,540 --> 00:03:44,019
If your language processor is engaged,

74
00:03:44,019 --> 00:03:46,480
browsing the web or reading your email,

75
00:03:46,480 --> 00:03:47,959
you're distracted.

76
00:03:47,959 --> 00:03:50,859
And worse yet, you distract all the people around you.

77
00:03:50,859 --> 00:03:52,500
Studies have shown that.

78
00:03:52,500 --> 00:03:54,500
And worse yet, if I see an open laptop,

79
00:03:54,500 --> 00:03:57,500
somewhere back there or up here,

80
00:03:57,500 --> 00:03:59,500
it drives me nuts.

81
00:03:59,500 --> 00:04:01,500
And I do a worse job.

82
00:04:01,500 --> 00:04:03,500
And so that ensures that all of your friends

83
00:04:03,500 --> 00:04:07,500
who are paying attention don't get the performance

84
00:04:07,500 --> 00:04:09,500
that they came to have.

85
00:04:09,500 --> 00:04:12,500
So that's it for preamble.

86
00:04:12,500 --> 00:04:14,500
Let's get started.

87
00:04:14,500 --> 00:04:17,000
First thing to talk about,

88
00:04:17,000 --> 00:04:19,500
of course, is how to start.

89
00:04:24,500 --> 00:04:27,500
Some people think the right thing to do

90
00:04:27,500 --> 00:04:32,500
is to start a talk with a joke.

91
00:04:39,500 --> 00:04:40,500
I don't recommend it.

92
00:04:43,500 --> 00:04:45,000
And the reason is that

93
00:04:45,000 --> 00:04:46,500
in the beginning of a talk,

94
00:04:46,500 --> 00:04:49,500
people are still putting their laptops away.

95
00:04:49,500 --> 00:04:52,500
They're becoming adjusted to your speaking parameters,

96
00:04:52,500 --> 00:04:53,500
to your vocal parameters.

97
00:04:54,500 --> 00:04:57,500
And they're not ready for a joke.

98
00:04:57,500 --> 00:04:59,500
So it doesn't work very well.

99
00:04:59,500 --> 00:05:01,500
They usually fall flat.

100
00:05:01,500 --> 00:05:04,000
What you want to do instead

101
00:05:04,000 --> 00:05:06,500
is start with an empowerment promise.

102
00:05:06,500 --> 00:05:11,500
You want to tell people what they're going to know

103
00:05:11,500 --> 00:05:14,500
at the end of the hour that they didn't know

104
00:05:14,500 --> 00:05:16,500
at the beginning of the hour.

105
00:05:16,500 --> 00:05:17,500
It's an empowerment promise.

106
00:05:17,500 --> 00:05:19,500
It's the reason for being here.

107
00:05:19,500 --> 00:05:22,500
What would be an example?

108
00:05:22,500 --> 00:05:23,500
Oh, I see.

109
00:05:23,500 --> 00:05:27,500
At the end of this 60 minutes,

110
00:05:27,500 --> 00:05:30,500
you will know things about speaking you don't know now.

111
00:05:30,500 --> 00:05:32,500
And something among those things you know

112
00:05:32,500 --> 00:05:34,500
will make a difference in your life.

113
00:05:34,500 --> 00:05:36,500
Yeah, that's an empowerment promise.

114
00:05:36,500 --> 00:05:38,500
So that's the best way to start.

115
00:05:38,500 --> 00:05:40,000
So now that I've talked a

116
00:05:40,000 --> 00:05:41,500
little bit about how to start,

117
00:05:41,500 --> 00:05:43,000
what I want to do is

118
00:05:43,000 --> 00:05:44,500
give you some samples of heuristics

119
00:05:44,500 --> 00:05:46,500
that are always on my

120
00:05:46,500 --> 00:05:48,500
mind when I give a talk.

121
00:05:48,500 --> 00:05:50,500
And the first of these heuristics

122
00:05:50,500 --> 00:05:52,500
is that it's a good idea

123
00:05:52,500 --> 00:05:56,500
to cycle on the subject.

124
00:05:56,500 --> 00:05:57,500
Go around it.

125
00:05:57,500 --> 00:05:58,500
Go around it again.

126
00:05:58,500 --> 00:06:00,500
Go around it again.

127
00:06:00,500 --> 00:06:03,000
Some people say, tell them

128
00:06:03,000 --> 00:06:05,500
what you want to tell them.

129
00:06:05,500 --> 00:06:07,500
Tell them again.

130
00:06:07,500 --> 00:06:08,500
And then tell them a third time,

131
00:06:08,500 --> 00:06:11,500
as if people weren't intelligent.

132
00:06:11,500 --> 00:06:13,500
But the point is, the reason

133
00:06:13,500 --> 00:06:15,500
is, well, there are many reasons,

134
00:06:15,500 --> 00:06:17,500
one of which is at any given moment,

135
00:06:17,500 --> 00:06:19,500
about 20% of you will be fogged

136
00:06:19,500 --> 00:06:21,500
out no matter what the lecture is.

137
00:06:21,500 --> 00:06:23,500
So if you want to ensure that the probability

138
00:06:23,500 --> 00:06:26,000
that everybody gets it is high,

139
00:06:26,000 --> 00:06:28,500
you need to say it three times.

140
00:06:28,500 --> 00:06:30,000
So cycling is one of the

141
00:06:30,000 --> 00:06:31,500
things that I always think about

142
00:06:31,500 --> 00:06:33,500
when I give a talk.

143
00:06:33,500 --> 00:06:37,500
Another thing I think about is in explaining my idea,

144
00:06:37,500 --> 00:06:44,500
I want to build a fence around it

145
00:06:44,500 --> 00:06:47,500
so that it's not confused with somebody else's idea.

146
00:06:47,500 --> 00:06:49,000
So if you were from

147
00:06:49,000 --> 00:06:50,500
Mars and I was teaching you

148
00:06:50,500 --> 00:06:52,500
about what an arch is, I might

149
00:06:52,500 --> 00:06:54,500
say to you, well, that's an arch.

150
00:06:54,500 --> 00:06:57,500
And that's not to be confused with some other things

151
00:06:57,500 --> 00:06:59,500
that other people might think is an arch.

152
00:06:59,500 --> 00:07:01,500
This is not an arch.

153
00:07:01,500 --> 00:07:03,500
That's not an arch.

154
00:07:03,500 --> 00:07:05,500
I'm building a fence around my idea

155
00:07:05,500 --> 00:07:08,500
so that it can be distinguished from somebody else's idea.

156
00:07:08,500 --> 00:07:10,500
So in a more technical sense, I might say,

157
00:07:10,500 --> 00:07:15,500
well, my algorithm might seem similar to Jones's algorithm,

158
00:07:15,500 --> 00:07:17,500
except his is exponential and mine's linear.

159
00:07:17,500 --> 00:07:19,500
That's putting a fence around your idea

160
00:07:19,500 --> 00:07:21,500
so that people can not be confused

161
00:07:21,500 --> 00:07:26,500
about how it might relate to something else.

162
00:07:26,500 --> 00:07:29,500
The third thing on this list of samples

163
00:07:29,500 --> 00:07:33,500
is the idea of verbal punctuation.

164
00:07:44,500 --> 00:07:48,500
And the idea here is that because people will occasionally

165
00:07:48,500 --> 00:07:52,500
fog out and need to get back on the bus,

166
00:07:52,500 --> 00:07:55,500
you need to provide some landmark places

167
00:07:55,500 --> 00:07:57,500
where you're announcing that it's a

168
00:07:57,500 --> 00:07:59,500
good time to get back on.

169
00:07:59,500 --> 00:08:02,500
So I might, in this talk,

170
00:08:02,500 --> 00:08:05,500
say something about this being my outline.

171
00:08:05,500 --> 00:08:07,000
The first thing we're going to

172
00:08:07,000 --> 00:08:08,500
do is talk about how to start.

173
00:08:08,500 --> 00:08:11,500
Then we're going to deal with these four samples.

174
00:08:11,500 --> 00:08:13,500
And among these four samples,

175
00:08:13,500 --> 00:08:16,500
I've talked about the first idea, that's cycling,

176
00:08:16,500 --> 00:08:17,500
the second idea, building a fence.

177
00:08:17,500 --> 00:08:21,500
And now the third idea is verbal punctuation.

178
00:08:21,500 --> 00:08:24,500
So I'm enumerating, I'm providing numbers,

179
00:08:24,500 --> 00:08:26,000
I'm giving you a sense that

180
00:08:26,000 --> 00:08:27,500
there's a seam in the talk

181
00:08:27,500 --> 00:08:30,500
and you can get back on.

182
00:08:30,500 --> 00:08:32,500
Okay?

183
00:08:32,500 --> 00:08:35,500
So now we're on a roll.

184
00:08:35,500 --> 00:08:37,500
And since we're on a roll,

185
00:08:37,500 --> 00:08:41,500
can you guess what fourth idea might be here?

186
00:08:41,500 --> 00:08:44,500
An idea that helps people get back on the bus?

187
00:08:47,500 --> 00:08:49,500
Ask a question.

188
00:08:49,500 --> 00:08:51,500
Yes.

189
00:08:51,500 --> 00:08:53,500
Ask a question.

190
00:08:53,500 --> 00:08:55,500
Ask a question, yes.

191
00:08:55,500 --> 00:08:57,500
Thank you.

192
00:08:57,500 --> 00:08:59,500
So ask a question.

193
00:09:05,500 --> 00:09:07,500
And so I will ask a question.

194
00:09:07,500 --> 00:09:09,500
How much dead air can there be?

195
00:09:09,500 --> 00:09:11,500
How long can I pause?

196
00:09:11,500 --> 00:09:13,500
I counted seven seconds.

197
00:09:13,500 --> 00:09:15,500
It seems like an eternity to me

198
00:09:15,500 --> 00:09:17,500
to wait and not say anything for seven seconds.

199
00:09:17,500 --> 00:09:19,500
But that's the standard amount of time

200
00:09:19,500 --> 00:09:20,500
you can wait for an answer.

201
00:09:20,500 --> 00:09:23,500
And of course the question has to be carefully chosen.

202
00:09:23,500 --> 00:09:24,500
It can't be too obvious

203
00:09:24,500 --> 00:09:26,500
because then people will be embarrassed

204
00:09:26,500 --> 00:09:27,500
to say what the answer is.

205
00:09:27,500 --> 00:09:28,500
It can't be too hard

206
00:09:28,500 --> 00:09:32,500
because then nobody will have anything to say.

207
00:09:32,500 --> 00:09:34,500
So here are some sample heuristics

208
00:09:34,500 --> 00:09:36,500
you can put in your armamentorium

209
00:09:36,500 --> 00:09:43,500
and build up your repertoire of ideas about presentation.

210
00:09:43,500 --> 00:09:46,500
And now if this persuades you

211
00:09:46,500 --> 00:09:49,500
that there is something to know,

212
00:09:49,500 --> 00:09:51,500
that there is knowledge,

213
00:09:51,500 --> 00:09:53,500
then I've already succeeded.

214
00:09:53,500 --> 00:09:55,500
Because what I want to convince you of

215
00:09:55,500 --> 00:09:57,500
is that if you watch the speakers you admire

216
00:09:57,500 --> 00:09:59,500
and feel are effective

217
00:09:59,500 --> 00:10:01,500
and ask yourselves why they're successful,

218
00:10:01,500 --> 00:10:05,500
then you can build up your own personal repertoire

219
00:10:05,500 --> 00:10:07,500
and develop your own personal style.

220
00:10:07,500 --> 00:10:09,500
And that's my fundamental objective.

221
00:10:09,500 --> 00:10:11,500
And the rest of this talk

222
00:10:11,500 --> 00:10:13,000
is about some of the

223
00:10:13,000 --> 00:10:14,500
things that are in my armamentorium

224
00:10:14,500 --> 00:10:15,500
that I think are effective.

225
00:10:15,500 --> 00:10:19,500
So next thing on our agenda

226
00:10:19,500 --> 00:10:22,500
as we start to discuss these other things

227
00:10:22,500 --> 00:10:24,500
is a discussion of time and place.

228
00:10:24,500 --> 00:10:26,000
So what do you think is

229
00:10:26,000 --> 00:10:27,500
a good time to have a lecture?

230
00:10:27,500 --> 00:10:31,500
11 a.m.

231
00:10:31,500 --> 00:10:33,500
Yeah.

232
00:10:37,500 --> 00:10:39,500
And the reason is

233
00:10:39,500 --> 00:10:43,500
most people at MIT are awake by then

234
00:10:43,500 --> 00:10:45,500
and hardly anyone is coming.

235
00:10:45,500 --> 00:10:47,500
So they're just going back to sleep.

236
00:10:47,500 --> 00:10:49,500
It's not right after a meal.

237
00:10:49,500 --> 00:10:52,500
People aren't fatigued from this or that.

238
00:10:52,500 --> 00:10:54,500
It's a great time to have a lecture.

239
00:10:54,500 --> 00:10:56,500
So that brings me next to the question of

240
00:10:56,500 --> 00:10:58,500
what about the place?

241
00:10:58,500 --> 00:11:00,500
And the most important thing about the place

242
00:11:00,500 --> 00:11:03,500
is that it be well lit.

243
00:11:08,500 --> 00:11:11,500
This room is well lit.

244
00:11:11,500 --> 00:11:13,500
The problem with other kinds of rooms

245
00:11:13,500 --> 00:11:15,500
is that we humans,

246
00:11:15,500 --> 00:11:17,500
whenever the lights go down,

247
00:11:17,500 --> 00:11:20,500
whenever the room is dimly lighted,

248
00:11:20,500 --> 00:11:22,500
it signals that we should go to sleep.

249
00:11:22,500 --> 00:11:24,500
So whenever I go somewhere to give a talk,

250
00:11:24,500 --> 00:11:25,500
even today,

251
00:11:25,500 --> 00:11:26,500
the first thing I do

252
00:11:26,500 --> 00:11:29,500
when I speak to the audiovisual people is say,

253
00:11:29,500 --> 00:11:31,500
keep the lights full up.

254
00:11:33,500 --> 00:11:36,500
Oh, they might reply,

255
00:11:36,500 --> 00:11:38,500
people will see the slides better

256
00:11:38,500 --> 00:11:40,500
if we turn the lights off.

257
00:11:40,500 --> 00:11:42,500
And then I reply,

258
00:11:42,500 --> 00:11:44,500
it's extremely hard to see slides through closed eyelids.

259
00:11:45,500 --> 00:11:46,500
So I'm not sure if that's

260
00:11:46,500 --> 00:11:47,500
the right way to do it.

261
00:11:47,500 --> 00:11:49,500
But I think it's a good idea.

262
00:11:49,500 --> 00:11:52,500
What else can you say about the place?

263
00:11:52,500 --> 00:11:54,500
Well, the place should be cased.

264
00:11:57,500 --> 00:12:00,500
And I mean that in a colloquial sense of,

265
00:12:00,500 --> 00:12:02,500
like if you're robbing a bank,

266
00:12:02,500 --> 00:12:04,500
you would go to the bank,

267
00:12:04,500 --> 00:12:06,500
you know, some occasions before

268
00:12:06,500 --> 00:12:08,500
to see what it's like,

269
00:12:08,500 --> 00:12:10,500
so there are no surprises

270
00:12:10,500 --> 00:12:12,500
when you do your robbery.

271
00:12:12,500 --> 00:12:14,500
So whenever I go somewhere to say,

272
00:12:14,500 --> 00:12:16,500
whenever I go somewhere to speak,

273
00:12:16,500 --> 00:12:18,500
the first thing I ask my host to do

274
00:12:18,500 --> 00:12:19,500
is to take me to

275
00:12:19,500 --> 00:12:20,500
the place where I'll be speaking

276
00:12:20,500 --> 00:12:22,500
so that if there are any weirdnesses,

277
00:12:22,500 --> 00:12:26,500
I'll be able to deal with it.

278
00:12:26,500 --> 00:12:29,500
Sometimes it might require some intervention.

279
00:12:29,500 --> 00:12:31,500
Sometimes it just might require me to

280
00:12:31,500 --> 00:12:33,500
understand what the challenges are.

281
00:12:33,500 --> 00:12:36,500
So when I came here this morning,

282
00:12:36,500 --> 00:12:39,500
I did what I typically do.

283
00:12:39,500 --> 00:12:41,500
I imagined that all the seats were filled

284
00:12:41,500 --> 00:12:43,500
with disinterested farm animals.

285
00:12:44,500 --> 00:12:46,500
And I just went away.

286
00:12:46,500 --> 00:12:48,500
I knew that no matter how bad it was,

287
00:12:48,500 --> 00:12:50,500
it wouldn't be as bad as that.

288
00:12:52,500 --> 00:12:58,500
So finally, it should be reasonably populated.

289
00:13:04,500 --> 00:13:07,500
It should be the case that,

290
00:13:07,500 --> 00:13:09,500
you know, if there were ten people in this hall,

291
00:13:09,500 --> 00:13:11,500
everyone would be wondering what's going on

292
00:13:11,500 --> 00:13:13,500
that's so much more interesting

293
00:13:13,500 --> 00:13:15,500
than just a few people sitting in one place.

294
00:13:15,500 --> 00:13:17,500
So you want to get a right-sized place

295
00:13:17,500 --> 00:13:19,500
that doesn't have to be packed,

296
00:13:19,500 --> 00:13:21,500
but it has to be more than half full.

297
00:13:21,500 --> 00:13:24,500
So those are some thoughts about time and place.

298
00:13:24,500 --> 00:13:26,500
The next thing I want to talk about

299
00:13:26,500 --> 00:13:31,500
is the subject of boards and props and slides.

300
00:13:38,500 --> 00:13:41,500
Well, these are the tools of the trade.

301
00:13:41,500 --> 00:13:46,500
I believe that this is the right tool

302
00:13:46,500 --> 00:13:51,500
for speaking when your purpose is informing.

303
00:13:51,500 --> 00:13:55,500
The slides are good when your purpose is exposing.

304
00:13:55,500 --> 00:13:57,500
But this is what I use when I'm informing,

305
00:13:57,500 --> 00:13:59,500
teaching, lecturing.

306
00:13:59,500 --> 00:14:02,500
And there are several reasons why I use it.

307
00:14:02,500 --> 00:14:04,500
For one thing, when you use the board,

308
00:14:04,500 --> 00:14:06,500
you have a graphic quality.

309
00:14:06,500 --> 00:14:09,500
It's the case that when you have,

310
00:14:09,500 --> 00:14:11,500
you know, a board,

311
00:14:11,500 --> 00:14:15,500
then you can easily exploit the fact

312
00:14:15,500 --> 00:14:20,500
that you can use graphics in your presentation.

313
00:14:20,500 --> 00:14:23,500
So that's the graphic quality that I like.

314
00:14:23,500 --> 00:14:25,000
And the next thing I like

315
00:14:25,000 --> 00:14:26,500
is the like a speed property.

316
00:14:26,500 --> 00:14:31,500
The speed with which you write on the blackboard

317
00:14:31,500 --> 00:14:33,500
is approximately the speed

318
00:14:33,500 --> 00:14:35,500
at which people can absorb ideas.

319
00:14:35,500 --> 00:14:37,500
If you go flipping through a bunch of slides,

320
00:14:37,500 --> 00:14:39,500
nobody can go that fast.

321
00:14:39,500 --> 00:14:40,500
So, I'm going to show you

322
00:14:40,500 --> 00:14:41,500
a little bit of a slide.

323
00:14:41,500 --> 00:14:46,500
Finally, one great property of a board

324
00:14:46,500 --> 00:14:53,500
is that it can be a target.

325
00:14:53,500 --> 00:14:58,500
Many people who are novices at speaking

326
00:14:58,500 --> 00:15:03,500
find themselves suddenly aware of their hands.

327
00:15:03,500 --> 00:15:05,500
It's as if their hands were private parts

328
00:15:05,500 --> 00:15:07,500
that shouldn't be exposed in public.

329
00:15:07,500 --> 00:15:10,500
So right away they go into the pockets.

330
00:15:10,500 --> 00:15:12,500
And this is considered insulting

331
00:15:12,500 --> 00:15:14,500
in some parts of the world.

332
00:15:14,500 --> 00:15:16,500
Or alternatively, maybe the hands will go

333
00:15:16,500 --> 00:15:19,500
in back like this.

334
00:15:19,500 --> 00:15:23,500
I was once in a convent in Serbia

335
00:15:23,500 --> 00:15:27,500
and my host, well, we were,

336
00:15:27,500 --> 00:15:29,000
as soon as we entered,

337
00:15:29,000 --> 00:15:30,500
a nun came up to us

338
00:15:30,500 --> 00:15:32,500
and offered us a refreshment.

339
00:15:32,500 --> 00:15:34,500
And I was about to say, no, thank you,

340
00:15:34,500 --> 00:15:38,500
when he said, eat that stuff or die.

341
00:15:38,500 --> 00:15:42,500
It's a question of local custom and politeness.

342
00:15:42,500 --> 00:15:45,500
But then, before anything happened there,

343
00:15:45,500 --> 00:15:49,500
the nun pulled my hands off like this

344
00:15:49,500 --> 00:15:51,500
because it was extraordinarily insulting

345
00:15:51,500 --> 00:15:54,500
in that culture to have your hands behind your back.

346
00:15:54,500 --> 00:15:56,500
So why is that?

347
00:15:56,500 --> 00:15:59,500
Well, it's usually supposed that it has to do

348
00:15:59,500 --> 00:16:02,500
with whether you're concealing a weapon.

349
00:16:02,500 --> 00:16:04,500
So if your hands are in your pockets

350
00:16:04,500 --> 00:16:08,500
or behind your back, then it looks like you

351
00:16:08,500 --> 00:16:09,500
have a weapon.

352
00:16:09,500 --> 00:16:11,500
And that's what I mean by the virtue,

353
00:16:11,500 --> 00:16:13,500
one of these virtues of the board.

354
00:16:13,500 --> 00:16:15,500
Now you have something to do with your hands.

355
00:16:15,500 --> 00:16:19,500
You can point out the stuff.

356
00:16:19,500 --> 00:16:21,500
I was once watching Seymour Papert give a lecture.

357
00:16:21,500 --> 00:16:23,500
I thought it was terrific.

358
00:16:23,500 --> 00:16:25,500
So I went a second time.

359
00:16:25,500 --> 00:16:27,500
First time to absorb the content,

360
00:16:27,500 --> 00:16:29,500
second time to note the style.

361
00:16:29,500 --> 00:16:31,500
And what I discovered is that Papert was constantly

362
00:16:31,500 --> 00:16:33,500
pointing at the board.

363
00:16:33,500 --> 00:16:35,500
And then I thought about it a little while

364
00:16:35,500 --> 00:16:37,000
and I noted that none of

365
00:16:37,000 --> 00:16:38,500
the stuff he was pointing to

366
00:16:38,500 --> 00:16:40,500
had anything to do with what he was saying.

367
00:16:42,500 --> 00:16:45,500
Nevertheless, it was an effective technique.

368
00:16:48,500 --> 00:16:50,500
So that's just a little

369
00:16:50,500 --> 00:16:52,500
bit about the virtue of blackboards.

370
00:16:52,500 --> 00:16:55,500
Now I want to talk about props.

371
00:16:55,500 --> 00:16:58,500
You know, the custodians of knowledge about props

372
00:16:58,500 --> 00:17:00,500
are the playwrights.

373
00:17:00,500 --> 00:17:05,500
Many decades ago, I saw a play by Heinrich Ibsen.

374
00:17:05,500 --> 00:17:07,500
It was Hedda Gabler.

375
00:17:07,500 --> 00:17:10,500
I remember vaguely that it was about a woman

376
00:17:10,500 --> 00:17:12,500
in an unhappy marriage.

377
00:17:12,500 --> 00:17:15,500
And her husband was in competition for an academic job

378
00:17:15,500 --> 00:17:17,500
with somebody else.

379
00:17:17,500 --> 00:17:19,500
And he was going to lose,

380
00:17:19,500 --> 00:17:21,500
partly because he was boring

381
00:17:21,500 --> 00:17:23,500
and partly because the competitor had just written

382
00:17:23,500 --> 00:17:25,500
a magnificent book.

383
00:17:25,500 --> 00:17:26,500
By the way, this was back

384
00:17:26,500 --> 00:17:27,500
in the days before there were

385
00:17:27,500 --> 00:17:29,500
copying machines and computers.

386
00:17:29,500 --> 00:17:32,500
Anyhow, as the play opens,

387
00:17:32,500 --> 00:17:35,500
there's a pot-bellied stove.

388
00:17:35,500 --> 00:17:37,000
And I'm going to show you

389
00:17:37,000 --> 00:17:38,500
a little bit of the story

390
00:17:38,500 --> 00:17:40,500
of the pot-bellied stove.

391
00:17:41,634 --> 00:17:43,522
ご視聴ありがとうございました

392
00:17:43,522 --> 00:17:48,022
And in the beginning of the play, the potbellied stove,

393
00:17:48,022 --> 00:17:53,022
with its open door, just has some slightly glowing embers.

394
00:17:54,262 --> 00:17:56,942
But the potbellied stove is always there,

395
00:17:56,942 --> 00:17:59,382
and its tension mounts in the play.

396
00:17:59,382 --> 00:18:02,262
And you see this manuscript, this prop,

397
00:18:02,262 --> 00:18:05,202
that Ibsen so artfully used, you just know

398
00:18:06,142 --> 00:18:08,462
that something's going to happen,

399
00:18:08,462 --> 00:18:10,902
because as the play goes on,

400
00:18:10,902 --> 00:18:13,801
the fire gets bigger and hotter,

401
00:18:13,801 --> 00:18:16,162
and finally, all-consuming, you just know

402
00:18:16,162 --> 00:18:20,022
that that manuscript is going to go into that fire.

403
00:18:21,162 --> 00:18:22,832
It's a memorable thing, it's

404
00:18:22,832 --> 00:18:24,502
what I remember about the play.

405
00:18:26,022 --> 00:18:29,302
So the playwrights have got this all figured out,

406
00:18:29,302 --> 00:18:31,822
but on the other hand, they're not the only people

407
00:18:31,822 --> 00:18:34,302
who can use props.

408
00:18:34,302 --> 00:18:37,522
Here's an example of the use of a prop,

409
00:18:37,522 --> 00:18:40,722
also due to Seymour Papert, he was talking about

410
00:18:40,902 --> 00:18:42,862
how it's important to look at

411
00:18:42,862 --> 00:18:44,822
the problem in the right way.

412
00:18:44,822 --> 00:18:48,302
And here's an example that not only teaches that,

413
00:18:48,302 --> 00:18:51,382
but makes it possible for you to embarrass your friends

414
00:18:51,382 --> 00:18:53,142
in mechanical engineering.

415
00:18:53,142 --> 00:18:55,062
So here's what you do.

416
00:18:55,062 --> 00:18:58,162
Take this bicycle wheel, and you start it spinning,

417
00:18:58,162 --> 00:19:01,962
and then you put some torque on the axle.

418
00:19:01,962 --> 00:19:06,202
Or, equivalently, you blow on the edge,

419
00:19:06,202 --> 00:19:08,722
and the issue is, does it go that way,

420
00:19:09,542 --> 00:19:10,842
or, does it go that way?

421
00:19:10,842 --> 00:19:16,042
Now the mechanical engineers will immediately say,

422
00:19:16,042 --> 00:19:18,242
oh yes, I see, right-hand screw wheel,

423
00:19:18,242 --> 00:19:20,921
and they'll put their fingers in this position,

424
00:19:20,921 --> 00:19:24,061
but forget exactly how to align their fingers

425
00:19:24,061 --> 00:19:26,522
with various aspects of the problem.

426
00:19:27,822 --> 00:19:29,121
And so it's usually the

427
00:19:29,121 --> 00:19:30,421
case that they get it right

428
00:19:30,421 --> 00:19:32,462
with about a 50% probability.

429
00:19:34,221 --> 00:19:36,671
So their very fancy education

430
00:19:36,671 --> 00:19:39,122
gets them up to the point

431
00:19:39,122 --> 00:19:40,742
where they're equivalent.

432
00:19:40,842 --> 00:19:41,682
And they're not.

433
00:19:41,682 --> 00:19:43,122
They're just flipping a coin.

434
00:19:43,122 --> 00:19:45,721
But it doesn't have to be that way,

435
00:19:45,721 --> 00:19:47,341
because you can think about

436
00:19:47,341 --> 00:19:48,962
the problem a little differently.

437
00:19:48,962 --> 00:19:50,022
So here's what you do.

438
00:19:50,902 --> 00:19:52,101
You take some duct tape,

439
00:19:53,082 --> 00:19:54,772
and you put it around the

440
00:19:54,772 --> 00:19:56,462
part of the wheel like that.

441
00:19:57,462 --> 00:19:58,982
And now you start to

442
00:19:58,982 --> 00:20:00,502
think about not the whole wheel,

443
00:20:00,502 --> 00:20:03,922
but just the little piece that's underneath the duct tape.

444
00:20:03,922 --> 00:20:06,702
So here that piece comes rolling over the top,

445
00:20:06,702 --> 00:20:08,192
and at this point you blow

446
00:20:08,192 --> 00:20:09,682
on it with a puff of air,

447
00:20:09,682 --> 00:20:10,682
forgetting about the rest of the wheel, and you're done.

448
00:20:10,682 --> 00:20:12,342
And the wheel, what happens to that little piece

449
00:20:12,342 --> 00:20:13,622
that's under the duct tape?

450
00:20:13,622 --> 00:20:15,022
It must want to go that way,

451
00:20:15,022 --> 00:20:17,242
because you banged on it like that.

452
00:20:17,242 --> 00:20:19,362
It's already going down like that.

453
00:20:19,362 --> 00:20:20,862
And what about the next piece?

454
00:20:20,862 --> 00:20:21,862
Same thing.

455
00:20:21,862 --> 00:20:23,041
Next piece, same thing.

456
00:20:23,041 --> 00:20:25,582
So the only thing that can happen

457
00:20:25,582 --> 00:20:27,962
is that the wheel goes over like that.

458
00:20:27,962 --> 00:20:30,362
And so now you'll never wonder again,

459
00:20:30,362 --> 00:20:32,962
because you're thinking about the problem in the right way,

460
00:20:32,962 --> 00:20:36,182
and it's demonstrated by the use of a prop.

461
00:20:38,922 --> 00:20:40,622
You can try this after we're done.

462
00:20:40,682 --> 00:20:47,682
Another example I like to remember

463
00:20:47,682 --> 00:20:50,682
is one from when I was taking 801.

464
00:20:50,682 --> 00:20:53,682
Alan Lazarus was the instructor at the time,

465
00:20:53,682 --> 00:20:56,682
and he was talking about the conservation of energy,

466
00:20:56,682 --> 00:20:58,682
kinetic and potential.

467
00:20:58,682 --> 00:21:01,182
And there was a long

468
00:21:01,182 --> 00:21:03,682
wire in the ceiling in 26100

469
00:21:03,682 --> 00:21:06,682
attached to a much bigger steel ball,

470
00:21:06,682 --> 00:21:08,922
but one not unlike this.

471
00:21:08,922 --> 00:21:10,682
And Lazarus said,

472
00:21:10,682 --> 00:21:15,082
I took the ball up against the wall like this.

473
00:21:15,082 --> 00:21:17,132
He put his head flat

474
00:21:17,132 --> 00:21:19,182
against the wall to steady himself,

475
00:21:19,182 --> 00:21:21,682
and then he let go.

476
00:21:21,682 --> 00:21:23,682
And the pendulum takes many

477
00:21:23,682 --> 00:21:25,682
seconds to go over and back,

478
00:21:25,682 --> 00:21:30,202
and then gently kisses Lazarus's nose.

479
00:21:31,202 --> 00:21:33,642
And so you have many seconds to think,

480
00:21:33,642 --> 00:21:36,682
this guy really believes in the conservation of energy.

481
00:21:40,682 --> 00:21:43,682
Do not try this at home.

482
00:21:43,682 --> 00:21:49,682
The problem is that the first time you do this,

483
00:21:49,682 --> 00:21:51,682
you may not just let go.

484
00:21:51,682 --> 00:21:54,682
There's a natural human tendency to push.

485
00:22:01,681 --> 00:22:04,681
So that's a little bit on the subject of props.

486
00:22:10,682 --> 00:22:25,682
You know, it's interesting.

487
00:22:25,682 --> 00:22:27,682
Whenever surveys are taken,

488
00:22:27,682 --> 00:22:31,682
students always say more chalk, less PowerPoint.

489
00:22:31,682 --> 00:22:33,682
And why would that be?

490
00:22:33,682 --> 00:22:36,682
Props are always also very effective.

491
00:22:36,682 --> 00:22:38,682
Why would that be?

492
00:22:38,682 --> 00:22:40,682
I'll give you my lunatic theory.

493
00:22:40,682 --> 00:22:43,682
I'll give you my lunatic fringe view on this.

494
00:22:43,682 --> 00:22:47,682
It has to do with what I would call

495
00:22:59,682 --> 00:23:01,682
empathetic mirroring.

496
00:23:01,682 --> 00:23:03,182
When you're sitting up there

497
00:23:03,182 --> 00:23:04,682
watching me right on the board,

498
00:23:04,682 --> 00:23:07,682
all those little mirror neurons in your head,

499
00:23:07,682 --> 00:23:09,682
I believe, become actuated,

500
00:23:09,682 --> 00:23:12,682
and you can feel yourself writing on the blackboard.

501
00:23:12,682 --> 00:23:14,682
And even more so,

502
00:23:14,682 --> 00:23:17,182
when I talk about this steel

503
00:23:17,182 --> 00:23:19,682
ball going that way and this way,

504
00:23:19,682 --> 00:23:23,682
you can feel the ball as if you were me.

505
00:23:23,682 --> 00:23:25,682
And you can't do that with a slide.

506
00:23:25,682 --> 00:23:27,682
You can't do it with a picture.

507
00:23:27,682 --> 00:23:30,682
You need to see it in a physical world.

508
00:23:30,682 --> 00:23:33,682
That's why I think that...

509
00:23:33,682 --> 00:23:37,682
Oh, yes, of course, there are speed questions involved, too,

510
00:23:37,682 --> 00:23:38,682
that have to be separated out.

511
00:23:38,682 --> 00:23:41,682
But I think that empathetic mirroring is why props

512
00:23:41,682 --> 00:23:46,682
and the use of a blackboard are so effective.

513
00:23:46,682 --> 00:23:50,682
Well, let's see.

514
00:23:50,682 --> 00:23:53,182
Oh, yes, there is one more

515
00:23:53,182 --> 00:23:55,682
thing by way of the tools,

516
00:23:55,682 --> 00:23:59,682
and that has to do with the use of slides.

517
00:23:59,682 --> 00:24:04,682
I repeat, I think they're for exposing ideas,

518
00:24:04,682 --> 00:24:05,682
not for teaching ideas,

519
00:24:05,682 --> 00:24:07,682
but that's what we do in a job talk

520
00:24:07,682 --> 00:24:10,682
or a conference talk.

521
00:24:10,682 --> 00:24:11,682
We expose ideas.

522
00:24:11,682 --> 00:24:13,682
We don't teach them.

523
00:24:13,682 --> 00:24:16,682
So let me tell you a

524
00:24:16,682 --> 00:24:19,682
little bit about my views on that.

525
00:24:19,682 --> 00:24:24,182
I remember once I was

526
00:24:24,182 --> 00:24:28,682
in Terminal A at Logan Airport.

527
00:24:28,682 --> 00:24:32,682
I'd just come back from a really miserable conference,

528
00:24:32,682 --> 00:24:34,682
and the flight was really horrible.

529
00:24:34,682 --> 00:24:36,182
It was one of those that

530
00:24:36,182 --> 00:24:37,682
feels like an unbalanced washing machine.

531
00:24:37,682 --> 00:24:39,682
And for the only time in my life,

532
00:24:39,682 --> 00:24:43,682
I decided to stop on my way to my car

533
00:24:43,682 --> 00:24:45,682
and have a cup of

534
00:24:45,682 --> 00:24:47,682
coffee and relax a little bit.

535
00:24:47,682 --> 00:24:49,682
And as I was there for a few minutes,

536
00:24:49,682 --> 00:24:51,682
someone came up to me and said,

537
00:24:51,682 --> 00:24:53,682
are you Professor Winston?

538
00:24:53,682 --> 00:24:55,682
I think so, I said.

539
00:24:55,682 --> 00:24:57,682
I don't know.

540
00:24:57,682 --> 00:24:59,682
I guess I was trying to be funny.

541
00:24:59,682 --> 00:25:01,682
In any event, he said, I'm on my

542
00:25:01,682 --> 00:25:03,682
way to Europe to give a job talk.

543
00:25:03,682 --> 00:25:06,682
Would you mind critiquing my slides?

544
00:25:06,682 --> 00:25:08,682
Not at all, I said.

545
00:25:08,682 --> 00:25:11,682
You have too many, and they have too many words.

546
00:25:11,682 --> 00:25:13,682
How did you know, he said,

547
00:25:13,682 --> 00:25:16,682
thinking perhaps I'd seen a talk of his before.

548
00:25:16,682 --> 00:25:18,682
I hadn't.

549
00:25:18,682 --> 00:25:21,682
My reply was, because it's always true.

550
00:25:21,682 --> 00:25:25,682
There are always too many slides, always too many words.

551
00:25:25,682 --> 00:25:28,182
So let me show you some extreme

552
00:25:28,182 --> 00:25:30,682
examples of how not to use slides.

553
00:25:33,682 --> 00:25:35,682
Well, for this demonstration,

554
00:25:35,682 --> 00:25:38,682
I need to be way over here.

555
00:25:41,682 --> 00:25:43,682
And when I get over here, then

556
00:25:43,682 --> 00:25:45,682
I can start to say things like,

557
00:25:47,682 --> 00:25:49,182
one of the things you

558
00:25:49,182 --> 00:25:50,682
shouldn't do is read your transparencies.

559
00:25:50,682 --> 00:25:52,682
People in your audience know how to read,

560
00:25:52,682 --> 00:25:54,682
and reading will just annoy them.

561
00:25:54,682 --> 00:25:56,682
Also, you should be sure that you

562
00:25:56,682 --> 00:25:58,682
have only a few words on each transparency

563
00:25:58,682 --> 00:26:01,682
and that the words are easy to read.

564
00:26:01,682 --> 00:26:03,682
And I hope they're driving you crazy.

565
00:26:03,682 --> 00:26:05,682
Because I'm committing, you know,

566
00:26:05,682 --> 00:26:07,682
committing all kinds of crimes.

567
00:26:07,682 --> 00:26:09,682
The first of which is that there

568
00:26:09,682 --> 00:26:11,682
are too many words on the slide.

569
00:26:11,682 --> 00:26:14,682
Second of which is I'm way over there,

570
00:26:14,682 --> 00:26:16,682
and the slide's way over there.

571
00:26:16,682 --> 00:26:19,682
So you get into this tennis match feeling

572
00:26:19,682 --> 00:26:22,182
of shifting back and forth

573
00:26:22,182 --> 00:26:24,682
between the slide and the speaker.

574
00:26:24,682 --> 00:26:26,182
You want the slides to

575
00:26:26,182 --> 00:26:27,682
be condiments to what you're saying,

576
00:26:27,682 --> 00:26:30,682
not the main event or the opposite way around.

577
00:26:30,682 --> 00:26:32,682
So how can we fix this?

578
00:26:32,682 --> 00:26:34,682
Step number one,

579
00:26:34,682 --> 00:26:37,682
is to get rid of the background junk.

580
00:26:37,682 --> 00:26:39,682
That's always distraction.

581
00:26:39,682 --> 00:26:42,682
Step number two is to get rid of the words.

582
00:26:42,682 --> 00:26:45,682
When I reduce the words to these,

583
00:26:45,682 --> 00:26:49,682
then everything I read a previous time,

584
00:26:49,682 --> 00:26:51,182
I'm not licensed to say

585
00:26:51,182 --> 00:26:52,682
because it's not on the slide.

586
00:26:52,682 --> 00:26:54,682
I'm not reading my slides anymore,

587
00:26:54,682 --> 00:26:57,182
but I'm saying what was written

588
00:26:57,182 --> 00:26:59,682
on the slides in a previous example.

589
00:27:01,682 --> 00:27:03,682
So what else can we do to simplify this?

590
00:27:04,682 --> 00:27:06,682
Well, we can get rid of the logos.

591
00:27:06,682 --> 00:27:08,682
We don't need them.

592
00:27:08,682 --> 00:27:11,682
Simplification.

593
00:27:11,682 --> 00:27:13,682
What else can we do?

594
00:27:13,682 --> 00:27:16,682
Get rid of the title.

595
00:27:16,682 --> 00:27:18,682
Now I want to talk to

596
00:27:18,682 --> 00:27:20,682
you about some rules for slide preparation.

597
00:27:20,682 --> 00:27:22,682
I'm telling you the title.

598
00:27:22,682 --> 00:27:24,682
It doesn't have to be up there.

599
00:27:24,682 --> 00:27:26,682
By reducing the number of words on the slide,

600
00:27:26,682 --> 00:27:28,682
I'm allowing you to pay more attention to me

601
00:27:28,682 --> 00:27:30,682
and less to what's written on the slide.

602
00:27:30,682 --> 00:27:32,682
I mentioned it before.

603
00:27:32,682 --> 00:27:34,682
We have only one language process.

604
00:27:34,682 --> 00:27:37,682
And we can either use it to read stuff

605
00:27:37,682 --> 00:27:39,682
or to listen to the speaker.

606
00:27:39,682 --> 00:27:41,182
And so if we have

607
00:27:41,182 --> 00:27:42,682
too many words on the slide,

608
00:27:42,682 --> 00:27:44,682
it forces people in the audience

609
00:27:44,682 --> 00:27:46,682
to read this stuff and not listen.

610
00:27:48,682 --> 00:27:50,182
A student of mine did

611
00:27:50,182 --> 00:27:51,682
an experiment a few years ago.

612
00:27:51,682 --> 00:27:59,682
He taught some students some web-based programming ideas.

613
00:27:59,682 --> 00:28:01,682
Half the information was on slides.

614
00:28:01,682 --> 00:28:03,682
He said the other half.

615
00:28:03,682 --> 00:28:06,682
And then for a control group, he reversed it.

616
00:28:06,682 --> 00:28:10,682
And the question was, what did the subjects,

617
00:28:10,682 --> 00:28:12,682
that is, a freshman in his fraternity,

618
00:28:12,682 --> 00:28:15,682
what did the subjects remember best?

619
00:28:15,682 --> 00:28:20,682
What he said or what they read on the slide?

620
00:28:20,682 --> 00:28:25,682
And the answer is what they read on the slide.

621
00:28:25,682 --> 00:28:27,682
When the slides have a lot of material on it,

622
00:28:27,682 --> 00:28:29,682
they don't pay attention to the speaker.

623
00:28:29,682 --> 00:28:31,682
In fact, in the after-action report,

624
00:28:31,682 --> 00:28:33,682
one of the subjects said,

625
00:28:33,682 --> 00:28:35,682
I wish you hadn't talked so much.

626
00:28:35,682 --> 00:28:36,682
It was distracting.

627
00:28:39,682 --> 00:28:42,682
Well, the last item is eliminate clutter.

628
00:28:42,682 --> 00:28:44,682
Now, here's some clutter.

629
00:28:44,682 --> 00:28:47,682
No reason even for those bullets.

630
00:28:47,682 --> 00:28:51,682
So the too-many-words problem is a consequence

631
00:28:51,682 --> 00:28:54,682
of a crime Microsoft has committed

632
00:28:54,682 --> 00:28:57,682
by allowing you to use fonts that are too small.

633
00:28:57,682 --> 00:29:02,682
So you should all have a sample slide like this

634
00:29:02,682 --> 00:29:04,682
that you can use to determine

635
00:29:04,682 --> 00:29:06,682
what the minimum font size is

636
00:29:06,682 --> 00:29:08,682
that's easily legible.

637
00:29:08,682 --> 00:29:12,682
Shibu, what do you think of those?

638
00:29:12,682 --> 00:29:16,682
What's that?

639
00:29:16,682 --> 00:29:18,682
Yeah.

640
00:29:18,682 --> 00:29:20,682
Minimum, maybe.

641
00:29:20,682 --> 00:29:23,682
Yeah, he says 40 or 50.

642
00:29:23,682 --> 00:29:25,682
I think that's about right.

643
00:29:25,682 --> 00:29:28,682
35 is if you can get too small.

644
00:29:28,682 --> 00:29:30,682
Not necessarily because you can't read it,

645
00:29:30,682 --> 00:29:32,682
but because you're probably using it

646
00:29:32,682 --> 00:29:34,682
to get too many words on the slide.

647
00:29:36,682 --> 00:29:38,682
What other crimes do we have?

648
00:29:38,682 --> 00:29:41,682
Well, we have the laser pointer crime.

649
00:29:43,682 --> 00:29:44,682
And for that, I brought a lot.

650
00:29:44,682 --> 00:29:46,682
You know, in the old days,

651
00:29:46,682 --> 00:29:47,682
when we didn't have laser pointers,

652
00:29:47,682 --> 00:29:48,682
we used wooden ones,

653
00:29:48,682 --> 00:29:51,682
and people would go waving these things around,

654
00:29:51,682 --> 00:29:56,682
and pretty soon it became almost like

655
00:29:56,682 --> 00:29:58,682
a baton swirling contest.

656
00:29:58,682 --> 00:30:00,682
So here's what we're doing.

657
00:30:00,682 --> 00:30:02,682
Here's what I recommended in the old days

658
00:30:02,682 --> 00:30:04,682
for dealing with this kind of pointer.

659
00:30:08,682 --> 00:30:10,682
This is an example of the use of a prop.

660
00:30:13,682 --> 00:30:15,182
Jim Glass up there saw

661
00:30:15,182 --> 00:30:16,682
this talk about 20 years ago

662
00:30:16,682 --> 00:30:18,682
and said, oh, yeah, I remember that talk.

663
00:30:18,682 --> 00:30:21,682
That's the one where you broke the pointer.

664
00:30:22,682 --> 00:30:24,682
It's amazing how props tend to be

665
00:30:24,682 --> 00:30:26,682
the things that are remembered.

666
00:30:26,682 --> 00:30:28,682
Well, now we don't have,

667
00:30:28,682 --> 00:30:30,682
we don't have,

668
00:30:30,682 --> 00:30:32,682
we don't have physical pointers anymore.

669
00:30:32,682 --> 00:30:34,682
We've got laser pointers.

670
00:30:34,682 --> 00:30:37,682
It's a wonder more people aren't driven

671
00:30:37,682 --> 00:30:40,682
into epileptic fits over this sort of stuff.

672
00:30:40,682 --> 00:30:43,682
But here's what tends to happen.

673
00:30:43,682 --> 00:30:44,682
Look at that.

674
00:30:44,682 --> 00:30:47,682
It's a lovely recursive picture,

675
00:30:47,682 --> 00:30:49,682
and I can become part of it

676
00:30:49,682 --> 00:30:51,682
by putting that laser beam

677
00:30:51,682 --> 00:30:53,682
right on the back of my head up there.

678
00:30:55,682 --> 00:30:57,682
And what do you see?

679
00:30:57,682 --> 00:30:59,682
You see the back of my head.

680
00:31:00,682 --> 00:31:03,682
I have no eye contact, no engagement, nothing.

681
00:31:03,682 --> 00:31:05,682
I was sitting with a

682
00:31:05,682 --> 00:31:07,682
student watching a talk one day,

683
00:31:07,682 --> 00:31:09,682
and she said, you know what?

684
00:31:09,682 --> 00:31:12,682
We could all leave, and he wouldn't know.

685
00:31:15,682 --> 00:31:17,682
So what happens when you use a laser pointer?

686
00:31:17,682 --> 00:31:19,682
You can't use a laser pointer without

687
00:31:19,682 --> 00:31:22,682
turning your head and pointing it at something.

688
00:31:22,682 --> 00:31:24,682
And when you do that,

689
00:31:24,682 --> 00:31:26,682
you lose contact with the audience.

690
00:31:26,682 --> 00:31:28,682
You don't want to do it.

691
00:31:28,682 --> 00:31:29,682
So what do you do if you,

692
00:31:29,682 --> 00:31:32,682
you need to identify something in your image,

693
00:31:32,682 --> 00:31:33,682
and you don't want to

694
00:31:33,682 --> 00:31:34,682
point at it with a laser?

695
00:31:34,682 --> 00:31:36,682
This is what you do.

696
00:31:36,682 --> 00:31:37,682
Put a little arrow on there and say,

697
00:31:37,682 --> 00:31:39,682
now look at that guy number one,

698
00:31:39,682 --> 00:31:41,682
at the end of arrow number one.

699
00:31:41,682 --> 00:31:42,682
You don't need to have

700
00:31:42,682 --> 00:31:43,682
a laser pointer to do that.

701
00:31:48,682 --> 00:31:50,682
The too heavy crime.

702
00:31:50,682 --> 00:31:53,682
When people ask me to review a presentation,

703
00:31:53,682 --> 00:31:55,682
I ask them to print it out

704
00:31:55,682 --> 00:31:57,682
and lay it out on a table.

705
00:31:59,682 --> 00:32:01,682
When they do that,

706
00:32:01,682 --> 00:32:04,682
it's easy to see whether the talk is too heavy,

707
00:32:04,682 --> 00:32:06,682
too much text,

708
00:32:06,682 --> 00:32:08,682
not enough air,

709
00:32:08,682 --> 00:32:09,682
not enough white space,

710
00:32:09,682 --> 00:32:11,682
not enough imagery.

711
00:32:11,682 --> 00:32:15,682
This is a good example of such a talk.

712
00:32:15,682 --> 00:32:17,682
Way too heavy.

713
00:32:17,682 --> 00:32:20,682
The presenter has taken advantage of

714
00:32:20,682 --> 00:32:22,682
small font sizes to get as

715
00:32:22,682 --> 00:32:24,682
much on the slide as he wanted.

716
00:32:26,682 --> 00:32:28,682
Lots of other crimes here,

717
00:32:28,682 --> 00:32:31,682
but the fact that it's too heavy

718
00:32:31,682 --> 00:32:33,682
is what I wanted to illustrate.

719
00:32:33,682 --> 00:32:34,682
So here, by contrast,

720
00:32:34,682 --> 00:32:36,682
another talk,

721
00:32:36,682 --> 00:32:37,682
one I gave a few years ago.

722
00:32:37,682 --> 00:32:40,682
It wasn't a deeply technical talk,

723
00:32:40,682 --> 00:32:43,682
but I show it to you because

724
00:32:43,682 --> 00:32:45,682
there's air in it.

725
00:32:45,682 --> 00:32:48,682
It's mostly pictures of things.

726
00:32:48,682 --> 00:32:50,182
There are three or four

727
00:32:50,182 --> 00:32:51,682
slides that have text on them,

728
00:32:51,682 --> 00:32:53,682
but when I come to those,

729
00:32:53,682 --> 00:32:55,682
I give the audience time to read them.

730
00:32:55,682 --> 00:32:57,682
And they're there because they might have

731
00:32:57,682 --> 00:32:59,682
some historical significance.

732
00:32:59,682 --> 00:33:01,682
The first slide with a lot of text on it

733
00:33:01,682 --> 00:33:04,682
is an extraction from the 1957,

734
00:33:04,682 --> 00:33:07,682
from the proposal for the 1957

735
00:33:07,682 --> 00:33:09,682
AI conference at Dartmouth.

736
00:33:09,682 --> 00:33:12,682
Extraordinarily interesting event,

737
00:33:12,682 --> 00:33:14,682
and that historical

738
00:33:14,682 --> 00:33:16,682
extraction from the proposal

739
00:33:16,682 --> 00:33:18,682
helps drive that point home.

740
00:33:19,682 --> 00:33:21,682
What else have we got here?

741
00:33:21,682 --> 00:33:24,682
Oh yeah, your vocabulary word for the day.

742
00:33:24,682 --> 00:33:26,682
This is an Apax Legomenon.

743
00:33:27,682 --> 00:33:29,682
What that means is,

744
00:33:29,682 --> 00:33:30,682
this is the kind of

745
00:33:30,682 --> 00:33:31,682
slide you can get away with

746
00:33:31,682 --> 00:33:33,682
exactly once in your presentation.

747
00:33:35,682 --> 00:33:37,182
This is a slide that

748
00:33:37,182 --> 00:33:38,682
got some currency some years ago

749
00:33:38,682 --> 00:33:41,682
because it shows the complexity

750
00:33:41,682 --> 00:33:44,682
of governing in Afghanistan

751
00:33:44,682 --> 00:33:48,682
by showing how impossibly complex it is.

752
00:33:48,682 --> 00:33:51,682
It's something you in the audience can't understand,

753
00:33:51,682 --> 00:33:53,682
and that's the point.

754
00:33:53,682 --> 00:33:55,682
But you can't have many of these.

755
00:33:55,682 --> 00:33:56,682
You can have one per work,

756
00:33:56,682 --> 00:33:57,682
one per presentation,

757
00:33:57,682 --> 00:33:58,682
one per paper,

758
00:33:58,682 --> 00:33:59,682
one per book.

759
00:33:59,682 --> 00:34:02,682
That's what Apax Legomenon is,

760
00:34:02,682 --> 00:34:04,682
and this is an example of it.

761
00:34:05,682 --> 00:34:07,682
Well, I'll show you some crimes,

762
00:34:07,682 --> 00:34:08,681
and so you might be asking,

763
00:34:08,681 --> 00:34:11,681
do these crimes actually occur?

764
00:34:11,681 --> 00:34:15,681
So, they do.

765
00:34:20,681 --> 00:34:22,681
There's the hands in the pockets crime.

766
00:34:22,681 --> 00:34:29,681
There's a crime and time and place selection here.

