and be sure to emphasize

819
00:37:04,182 --> 00:37:06,682
what it takes to inspire students?

820
00:37:06,682 --> 00:37:08,682
And strangely, I hadn't thought about that question before,

821
00:37:08,682 --> 00:37:10,682
so I started a survey.

822
00:37:10,682 --> 00:37:13,682
I talked to some of my incoming freshman advisees,

823
00:37:13,682 --> 00:37:17,682
and I talked to senior faculty and everything in between

824
00:37:17,682 --> 00:37:19,682
about how they've been inspired by this.

825
00:37:19,682 --> 00:37:22,682
And what I found from the incoming freshman

826
00:37:22,682 --> 00:37:25,682
is that they were inspired by some high school teacher

827
00:37:25,682 --> 00:37:27,682
who told them they could do it.

828
00:37:27,682 --> 00:37:30,682
What I found in the senior faculty,

829
00:37:30,682 --> 00:37:34,682
they were inspired by someone who helped them

830
00:37:34,682 --> 00:37:36,682
to see a problem in a new way.

831
00:37:36,682 --> 00:37:39,182
And what I saw from

832
00:37:39,182 --> 00:37:41,682
everyone is that they were inspired

833
00:37:41,682 --> 00:37:47,682
when someone exhibited passion about what they were doing.

834
00:37:47,682 --> 00:37:48,682
I used that.

835
00:37:48,682 --> 00:37:51,682
Exhibit passion about what they were doing.

836
00:37:51,682 --> 00:37:56,682
Yeah, so that's one way to be inspiring.

837
00:37:56,682 --> 00:37:59,682
It's easy for me because, you know,

838
00:37:59,682 --> 00:38:01,682
I do artificial intelligence.

839
00:38:01,682 --> 00:38:05,682
And how can you not be interested

840
00:38:05,682 --> 00:38:07,682
in artificial intelligence, right?

841
00:38:07,682 --> 00:38:10,682
I mean, if you're not interested in artificial intelligence,

842
00:38:10,682 --> 00:38:12,682
you're probably not interested in interesting things.

843
00:38:15,682 --> 00:38:17,682
So when I'm lecturing in my,

844
00:38:17,682 --> 00:38:19,682
in my AI class,

845
00:38:19,682 --> 00:38:21,182
it's natural for me to talk

846
00:38:21,182 --> 00:38:22,682
about what I think is cool

847
00:38:22,682 --> 00:38:26,682
and how exciting some new idea is.

848
00:38:26,682 --> 00:38:28,682
So that's the kind of,

849
00:38:28,682 --> 00:38:30,682
that's the kind of expression of passion

850
00:38:30,682 --> 00:38:33,682
that makes a difference while informing

851
00:38:33,682 --> 00:38:37,682
with respect to this question of inspiring.

852
00:38:39,682 --> 00:38:42,682
Oh, yeah, and of course, during this promise phase,

853
00:38:42,682 --> 00:38:46,682
you can also express how cool stuff is.

854
00:38:46,682 --> 00:38:48,682
Let me give you an example of a lecture

855
00:38:48,682 --> 00:38:50,682
that starts this way.

856
00:38:50,682 --> 00:38:52,682
I'm talking about resource allocation.

857
00:38:52,682 --> 00:38:53,682
It's the same sort of stuff

858
00:38:53,682 --> 00:38:54,682
you would think of when you're,

859
00:38:54,682 --> 00:38:56,682
it's the same sort of ideas you would need

860
00:38:56,682 --> 00:39:00,682
if you're allocating aircraft to a flight schedule

861
00:39:00,682 --> 00:39:03,682
or trying to schedule a factory or something like that.

862
00:39:03,682 --> 00:39:06,682
But the example is putting colors on the states

863
00:39:06,682 --> 00:39:09,682
in the United States without any bordering states

864
00:39:09,682 --> 00:39:10,682
having the same color.

865
00:39:10,682 --> 00:39:12,682
So here goes.

866
00:39:14,682 --> 00:39:16,682
This is what I show in my,

867
00:39:16,682 --> 00:39:18,682
in the beginning of the class.

868
00:39:18,682 --> 00:39:20,682
This is a way of doing that coloring.

869
00:39:20,682 --> 00:39:21,682
And you might say,

870
00:39:21,682 --> 00:39:24,682
well, why don't we wait till it finishes?

871
00:39:24,682 --> 00:39:26,682
Would you like to do that?

872
00:39:26,682 --> 00:39:28,682
No?

873
00:39:28,682 --> 00:39:30,682
Well, we're not gonna wait till it finishes

874
00:39:30,682 --> 00:39:32,682
because the sun will have exploded

875
00:39:32,682 --> 00:39:35,682
and consumed the earth before this program finishes.

876
00:39:36,682 --> 00:39:40,682
But with a slight adjustment to how the program works,

877
00:39:40,682 --> 00:39:41,682
which I tell my students,

878
00:39:41,682 --> 00:39:44,682
you will understand in the next 50 minutes,

879
00:39:44,682 --> 00:39:46,682
this is what you get.

880
00:39:46,682 --> 00:39:50,682
Isn't that cool?

881
00:39:50,682 --> 00:39:53,682
You know, you gotta be amazed by stuff

882
00:39:53,682 --> 00:39:57,682
that takes a computation from longer than the lifetime

883
00:39:57,682 --> 00:39:59,682
of the solar system into a few seconds.

884
00:39:59,682 --> 00:40:02,182
So that's what I mean

885
00:40:02,182 --> 00:40:04,682
by providing a promise up front

886
00:40:04,682 --> 00:40:08,682
and expressing some passion about what you're talking about.

887
00:40:08,682 --> 00:40:11,682
Well, the last item in this little block here

888
00:40:11,682 --> 00:40:13,682
has to do with what people

889
00:40:13,682 --> 00:40:15,682
think that they do at MIT.

890
00:40:15,682 --> 00:40:18,682
You ask faculty what the most important purpose is,

891
00:40:18,682 --> 00:40:20,682
and they'll say, well,

892
00:40:20,682 --> 00:40:22,682
the most important thing I do

893
00:40:22,682 --> 00:40:24,682
is teach people how to think.

894
00:40:24,682 --> 00:40:27,682
And then you say, oh, that's great.

895
00:40:27,682 --> 00:40:30,682
How do you teach people how to think?

896
00:40:30,682 --> 00:40:33,682
Blank stare.

897
00:40:33,682 --> 00:40:37,682
No one can quite respond to that part,

898
00:40:37,682 --> 00:40:39,682
that natural next question.

899
00:40:39,682 --> 00:40:41,682
So how do you teach people how to think?

900
00:40:41,682 --> 00:40:44,682
Well, I believe that we all have a purpose.

901
00:40:44,682 --> 00:40:48,682
I believe that we are storytelling animals

902
00:40:48,682 --> 00:40:51,682
and that we start developing our story,

903
00:40:51,682 --> 00:40:53,682
understanding and manipulating skills

904
00:40:53,682 --> 00:40:55,682
with fairy tales in childhood

905
00:40:55,682 --> 00:40:58,682
and continue on through professional schools

906
00:40:58,682 --> 00:41:03,682
like law, business, medicine, engineering, everything.

907
00:41:03,682 --> 00:41:06,682
And we continue doing that throughout life.

908
00:41:06,682 --> 00:41:09,682
So if that is what thinking is all about,

909
00:41:09,682 --> 00:41:11,682
then when you want to teach people how to think,

910
00:41:11,682 --> 00:41:13,682
you provide them with the stories they need to know,

911
00:41:13,682 --> 00:41:16,682
the questions they need to ask about those stories,

912
00:41:16,682 --> 00:41:18,682
mechanisms for analyzing those stories,

913
00:41:18,682 --> 00:41:20,682
ways of putting stories together,

914
00:41:20,682 --> 00:41:24,682
ways of evaluating how reliable a story is,

915
00:41:24,682 --> 00:41:26,682
and that's what I think you need to do

916
00:41:26,682 --> 00:41:29,682
when you teach people how to think.

917
00:41:29,682 --> 00:41:31,682
But that's all about education,

918
00:41:31,682 --> 00:41:34,682
and many of you here,

919
00:41:34,682 --> 00:41:36,682
not necessarily for that,

920
00:41:36,682 --> 00:41:39,682
but rather for this part,

921
00:41:39,682 --> 00:41:40,682
for persuading,

922
00:41:40,682 --> 00:41:42,682
which breaks down into several categories,

923
00:41:42,682 --> 00:41:44,682
oral exams not shown,

924
00:41:44,682 --> 00:41:47,682
job talks getting famous.

925
00:41:47,682 --> 00:41:49,682
I won't say much about oral exams

926
00:41:49,682 --> 00:41:51,682
other than the fact that they used to be

927
00:41:51,682 --> 00:41:54,682
a lot scarier than they are today.

928
00:41:54,682 --> 00:41:57,682
In the old days,

929
00:41:57,682 --> 00:41:59,682
reading the literature in a foreign language

930
00:41:59,682 --> 00:42:00,682
was part of that,

931
00:42:00,682 --> 00:42:03,682
and there was a high failure rate.

932
00:42:03,682 --> 00:42:07,682
And when you look back on those failures,

933
00:42:07,682 --> 00:42:11,682
the most usual reason for people failing an oral exam

934
00:42:11,682 --> 00:42:16,682
is failure to situate and failure to practice.

935
00:42:16,682 --> 00:42:19,682
By situate, I mean it's important to talk about

936
00:42:19,682 --> 00:42:21,682
your research in context.

937
00:42:21,682 --> 00:42:23,182
This is a problem that's

938
00:42:23,182 --> 00:42:24,682
being pursued all over the world.

939
00:42:24,682 --> 00:42:26,682
There hasn't been any progress before me

940
00:42:26,682 --> 00:42:29,682
in the past 30 years.

941
00:42:29,682 --> 00:42:31,682
Everyone is looking for a solution

942
00:42:31,682 --> 00:42:34,682
because it will have impact on so many other things.

943
00:42:34,682 --> 00:42:37,682
So that's situating in time and place and feel.

944
00:42:37,682 --> 00:42:40,682
And then as far as practice is concerned,

945
00:42:40,682 --> 00:42:42,682
yes, practice is important,

946
00:42:42,682 --> 00:42:46,682
but that doesn't mean showing your slides

947
00:42:46,682 --> 00:42:49,682
to the people you share an office with.

948
00:42:49,682 --> 00:42:51,682
The problem with that is that

949
00:42:51,682 --> 00:42:53,682
if people know what you're doing,

950
00:42:53,682 --> 00:42:55,682
they will hallucinate that there's material

951
00:42:55,682 --> 00:42:58,682
in your presentation that isn't there.

952
00:42:58,682 --> 00:43:00,682
A variation on this thing, by the way,

953
00:43:00,682 --> 00:43:04,682
is your faculty supervisor

954
00:43:04,682 --> 00:43:06,182
is not a very good person

955
00:43:06,182 --> 00:43:07,682
to help you debug a talk

956
00:43:07,682 --> 00:43:09,682
because they, in fact, know what you're doing,

957
00:43:09,682 --> 00:43:11,682
and they will, in fact, hallucinate

958
00:43:11,682 --> 00:43:14,682
there's material in your presentation that isn't there.

959
00:43:14,682 --> 00:43:16,682
So you need to get together some friends

960
00:43:16,682 --> 00:43:19,682
who don't know what you're doing and have them...

961
00:43:19,682 --> 00:43:22,682
Well, you start the practice session by saying,

962
00:43:22,682 --> 00:43:25,682
if you can't make me cry,

963
00:43:25,682 --> 00:43:28,682
I won't value you as a friend anymore.

964
00:43:28,682 --> 00:43:30,682
And then when you get to the faculty

965
00:43:30,682 --> 00:43:34,682
on an oral exam, it will be easy.

966
00:43:34,682 --> 00:43:37,682
You see, difficulty...

967
00:43:37,682 --> 00:43:39,682
The amount of flak you'll get from somebody

968
00:43:39,682 --> 00:43:41,682
is proportional to age.

969
00:43:41,682 --> 00:43:43,682
The older somebody is,

970
00:43:43,682 --> 00:43:46,682
the more they understand where they are in the world.

971
00:43:46,682 --> 00:43:47,682
But the young people are

972
00:43:47,682 --> 00:43:48,682
trying to show the old people

973
00:43:48,682 --> 00:43:51,682
how smart they are, so they'll be vicious.

974
00:43:51,682 --> 00:43:53,682
So whenever you have an opportunity

975
00:43:53,682 --> 00:43:54,682
to have an examining committee

976
00:43:54,682 --> 00:43:56,682
that's full of people with gray hair,

977
00:43:56,682 --> 00:43:58,682
that's what you want.

978
00:43:58,682 --> 00:44:00,682
Well, that's just a word or two

979
00:44:00,682 --> 00:44:02,682
about something I haven't listed here.

980
00:44:02,682 --> 00:44:04,682
Let's get into the subject of job talks.

981
00:44:04,682 --> 00:44:09,682
So I was sitting in a bar

982
00:44:09,682 --> 00:44:13,682
many years ago in San Diego.

983
00:44:13,682 --> 00:44:16,682
I was a member of the Navy Science Board,

984
00:44:16,682 --> 00:44:18,682
and I was sitting with a

985
00:44:18,682 --> 00:44:20,682
couple of my colleagues on the board.

986
00:44:20,682 --> 00:44:23,682
Dolores Eder from the University of Colorado.

987
00:44:23,682 --> 00:44:25,682
She made me so jealous I could spit

988
00:44:25,682 --> 00:44:27,682
because she'd written 21 books

989
00:44:27,682 --> 00:44:29,682
and I'd only written 17.

990
00:44:29,682 --> 00:44:33,682
And then the other one was

991
00:44:33,682 --> 00:44:37,682
Bill Weldon from the University of Texas.

992
00:44:37,682 --> 00:44:39,682
He was an electromagnet.

993
00:44:39,682 --> 00:44:41,682
He was a magnetism guy,

994
00:44:41,682 --> 00:44:43,682
and he knew how to use rail guns

995
00:44:43,682 --> 00:44:45,682
to drive steel rods through tank armor.

996
00:44:45,682 --> 00:44:47,682
These were interesting people.

997
00:44:47,682 --> 00:44:49,682
So I said, what do you look for

998
00:44:49,682 --> 00:44:52,682
in a faculty candidate?

999
00:44:52,682 --> 00:44:56,682
And within one microsecond,

1000
00:44:56,682 --> 00:44:58,682
Dolores said,

1001
00:44:58,682 --> 00:45:00,182
they have to show us

1002
00:45:00,182 --> 00:45:01,682
they've got some kind of vision.

1003
00:45:01,682 --> 00:45:04,682
Quickly followed by Bill,

1004
00:45:04,682 --> 00:45:06,682
who said, they have to show us

1005
00:45:06,682 --> 00:45:08,682
that they've done something.

1006
00:45:09,682 --> 00:45:17,682
Oh, that sounds good, I said.

1007
00:45:17,682 --> 00:45:19,682
And then I said to them,

1008
00:45:19,682 --> 00:45:21,682
how long does a candidate have

1009
00:45:21,682 --> 00:45:23,682
to establish these two things?

1010
00:45:23,682 --> 00:45:25,682
What do you think?

1011
00:45:25,682 --> 00:45:28,682
Well, compare your answer to theirs.

1012
00:45:32,682 --> 00:45:35,682
Five minutes.

1013
00:45:35,682 --> 00:45:38,682
So if you haven't expressed your vision,

1014
00:45:38,682 --> 00:45:40,682
if you haven't told people

1015
00:45:40,682 --> 00:45:42,682
that you've done something,

1016
00:45:42,682 --> 00:45:45,682
in five minutes you've already lost.

1017
00:45:45,682 --> 00:45:47,682
So you have to be able to do that.

1018
00:45:47,682 --> 00:45:49,682
And let me just mention a couple of things

1019
00:45:49,682 --> 00:45:51,682
in that connection.

1020
00:45:51,682 --> 00:45:53,682
Here, which is, you know, the vision

1021
00:45:53,682 --> 00:45:57,682
is in part a problem that somebody cares about

1022
00:45:57,682 --> 00:46:00,682
and something new in your approach.

1023
00:46:00,682 --> 00:46:07,682
So the problem is understanding

1024
00:46:07,682 --> 00:46:09,682
the nature of human intelligence.

1025
00:46:09,682 --> 00:46:12,682
And the approach is asking questions

1026
00:46:12,682 --> 00:46:14,682
about what makes us different

1027
00:46:14,682 --> 00:46:17,682
from chimpanzees and Neanderthals.

1028
00:46:17,682 --> 00:46:19,682
Is it merely a matter of quantity

1029
00:46:19,682 --> 00:46:21,682
or are we just a little bit smarter

1030
00:46:21,682 --> 00:46:23,682
in some continuous way?

1031
00:46:23,682 --> 00:46:26,682
Or do we have something that's fundamentally different

1032
00:46:26,682 --> 00:46:28,682
that chimpanzees don't have

1033
00:46:28,682 --> 00:46:29,682
and Neanderthals either?

1034
00:46:29,682 --> 00:46:31,682
And the answer is yes.

1035
00:46:31,682 --> 00:46:33,682
We do have something different.

1036
00:46:33,682 --> 00:46:35,682
We are symbolic creatures.

1037
00:46:35,682 --> 00:46:38,682
And because we're symbolic creatures,

1038
00:46:38,682 --> 00:46:43,682
we can build symbolic descriptions

1039
00:46:43,682 --> 00:46:45,682
of relations and events.

1040
00:46:45,682 --> 00:46:47,682
We can string them together and make stories.

1041
00:46:47,682 --> 00:46:49,682
And because we can make stories,

1042
00:46:49,682 --> 00:46:51,682
that's what makes us different.

1043
00:46:51,682 --> 00:46:53,682
So that's my stump speech.

1044
00:46:53,682 --> 00:46:55,682
That's how I start most of my talks

1045
00:46:55,682 --> 00:46:57,682
on my own personal research.

1046
00:46:57,682 --> 00:46:58,682
How do you,

1047
00:46:58,682 --> 00:47:01,682
how do you express the notion

1048
00:47:01,682 --> 00:47:03,682
that you've done something?

1049
00:47:03,682 --> 00:47:08,682
By listing the steps that need to be taken

1050
00:47:08,682 --> 00:47:11,682
in order to achieve the solution to that problem.

1051
00:47:11,682 --> 00:47:13,682
You don't have to have done all of those steps,

1052
00:47:13,682 --> 00:47:14,682
but you can say,

1053
00:47:14,682 --> 00:47:16,682
here's what needs to be done.

1054
00:47:16,682 --> 00:47:18,682
An example.

1055
00:47:18,682 --> 00:47:20,682
Here's what needs to be done.

1056
00:47:20,682 --> 00:47:22,682
We need to specify some behavior.

1057
00:47:22,682 --> 00:47:25,682
We need to enumerate the constraints

1058
00:47:25,682 --> 00:47:27,682
that make it possible to deal with that behavior.

1059
00:47:27,682 --> 00:47:29,682
We have to implement a system

1060
00:47:29,682 --> 00:47:30,682
because we're engineers

1061
00:47:30,682 --> 00:47:32,682
and we don't think that we've understood something

1062
00:47:32,682 --> 00:47:34,682
unless we can build it.

1063
00:47:34,682 --> 00:47:35,682
And we've built such a system

1064
00:47:35,682 --> 00:47:37,682
and we're about to demonstrate it to you today.

1065
00:47:37,682 --> 00:47:39,682
That would be an example of enumerating

1066
00:47:39,682 --> 00:47:43,682
a series of steps needed to realize the vision.

1067
00:47:43,682 --> 00:47:45,682
So then, blah, blah, blah,

1068
00:47:45,682 --> 00:47:47,682
blah, blah, blah, blah, blah, blah.

1069
00:47:47,682 --> 00:47:50,682
And then you conclude by,

1070
00:47:50,682 --> 00:47:54,682
you conclude by enumerating your contributions.

1071
00:47:57,682 --> 00:48:03,682
It's kind of a mirror of these steps

1072
00:48:03,682 --> 00:48:06,682
and helps to establish that you've done something.

1073
00:48:06,682 --> 00:48:08,682
So that's a kind of general purpose framework

1074
00:48:08,682 --> 00:48:11,682
for doing a technical talk.

1075
00:48:11,682 --> 00:48:15,682
Now, only a few more things left to do today.

1076
00:48:15,682 --> 00:48:18,682
Getting famous is the next item on our agenda

1077
00:48:18,682 --> 00:48:20,682
because once you've got the job,

1078
00:48:20,682 --> 00:48:22,682
you need to think a little bit about

1079
00:48:22,682 --> 00:48:25,682
how you're going to be recognized for what you do.

1080
00:48:25,682 --> 00:48:26,682
So.

1081
00:48:33,682 --> 00:48:34,682
Well, first of all,

1082
00:48:34,682 --> 00:48:37,682
why should you care about getting famous?

1083
00:48:37,682 --> 00:48:43,682
I thought about this in connection with a fundraising event

1084
00:48:43,682 --> 00:48:47,682
I attended once.

1085
00:48:47,682 --> 00:48:50,682
Fundraising event for raising money to save Venice

1086
00:48:50,682 --> 00:48:53,682
from going underwater and having all of its art destroyed.

1087
00:48:53,682 --> 00:48:56,682
Anyway, I was sitting here.

1088
00:48:56,682 --> 00:48:59,682
And JC was sitting here.

1089
00:48:59,682 --> 00:49:02,682
That was Julia, the late Julia Child.

1090
00:49:02,682 --> 00:49:09,682
And as the evening wore on,

1091
00:49:09,682 --> 00:49:11,682
more and more people would come up

1092
00:49:11,682 --> 00:49:14,682
and ask Julia to autograph something

1093
00:49:14,682 --> 00:49:19,682
or express a feeling that she had changed their life.

1094
00:49:19,682 --> 00:49:22,682
And it just happened over and over again.

1095
00:49:22,682 --> 00:49:24,682
So eventually, I turned to Julia and I said,

1096
00:49:24,682 --> 00:49:29,682
Ms. Child, is it fun to be famous?

1097
00:49:29,682 --> 00:49:31,182
And she thought about it

1098
00:49:31,182 --> 00:49:32,682
for a second and she said,

1099
00:49:32,682 --> 00:49:34,682
you get used to it.

1100
00:49:34,682 --> 00:49:37,682
But you know what occurred to me?

1101
00:49:37,682 --> 00:49:40,682
You never get used to being ignored.

1102
00:49:40,682 --> 00:49:43,682
So it's, you know, it's, it's. . .

1103
00:49:43,682 --> 00:49:45,682
Here's a way to think about it.

1104
00:49:45,682 --> 00:49:47,682
Your ideas are like your children.

1105
00:49:47,682 --> 00:49:48,682
And you don't want them to

1106
00:49:48,682 --> 00:49:49,682
go into the world in rags.

1107
00:49:49,682 --> 00:49:51,182
So what you want to do is

1108
00:49:51,182 --> 00:49:52,682
to be sure that you have these techniques,

1109
00:49:52,682 --> 00:49:55,182
these mechanisms, these thoughts about how

1110
00:49:55,182 --> 00:49:57,682
to present the ideas that you have

1111
00:49:57,682 --> 00:49:59,682
so that they're recognized for

1112
00:49:59,682 --> 00:50:01,682
the value that is in them.

1113
00:50:01,682 --> 00:50:03,682
So that's why it's a legitimate thing

1114
00:50:03,682 --> 00:50:06,682
to concern yourself with packaging.

1115
00:50:06,682 --> 00:50:09,682
Now, how do you get remembered?

1116
00:50:09,682 --> 00:50:13,682
Well, there's something I like to call Winston's Star.

1117
00:50:13,682 --> 00:50:16,182
And every one of the items

1118
00:50:16,182 --> 00:50:18,682
I'm about to articulate has a,

1119
00:50:18,682 --> 00:50:20,682
starts with an S.

1120
00:50:20,682 --> 00:50:22,682
So if you want your name to be remembered,

1121
00:50:22,682 --> 00:50:25,682
if you want your presentation ideas to be remembered,

1122
00:50:25,682 --> 00:50:27,182
one of the things you need to

1123
00:50:27,182 --> 00:50:28,682
do is to make sure that you have

1124
00:50:28,682 --> 00:50:33,682
some kind of symbol associated with your work.

1125
00:50:33,682 --> 00:50:36,682
So this arch example is actually from

1126
00:50:36,682 --> 00:50:39,682
my PhD thesis many, many years ago.

1127
00:50:39,682 --> 00:50:44,682
And in the course of my work at that time,

1128
00:50:44,682 --> 00:50:47,182
this work on arch learning became

1129
00:50:47,182 --> 00:50:49,682
mildly famous and I didn't know why.

1130
00:50:49,682 --> 00:50:51,682
It was only many years later that I realized that

1131
00:50:51,682 --> 00:50:52,682
that work was really important to me.

1132
00:50:52,682 --> 00:50:54,182
And I realized that this work accidentally

1133
00:50:54,182 --> 00:50:55,682
had all of the elements on this star.

1134
00:50:55,682 --> 00:50:57,682
So the first element is that

1135
00:50:57,682 --> 00:50:59,682
there was a kind of symbol.

1136
00:50:59,682 --> 00:51:04,682
It's the arch itself.

1137
00:51:04,682 --> 00:51:10,682
Next thing you need is some kind of slogan.

1138
00:51:10,682 --> 00:51:12,182
A kind of phrase that

1139
00:51:12,182 --> 00:51:13,682
provides a handle on the work.

1140
00:51:13,682 --> 00:51:15,682
And in this case, the phrase was,

1141
00:51:15,682 --> 00:51:19,682
one shot learning.

1142
00:51:19,682 --> 00:51:21,682
And it was one shot

1143
00:51:21,682 --> 00:51:24,682
because the program I wrote learned something definite

1144
00:51:24,682 --> 00:51:28,682
from every example that was presented to us.

1145
00:51:28,682 --> 00:51:32,682
So in going from a model based on this configuration

1146
00:51:32,682 --> 00:51:35,682
to something that isn't an arch based on that configuration,

1147
00:51:35,682 --> 00:51:38,682
the program learned that it has to be on top.

1148
00:51:38,682 --> 00:51:41,682
One shot learning.

1149
00:51:41,682 --> 00:51:45,682
So that's a symbol, slogan.

1150
00:51:45,682 --> 00:51:48,682
Now we need a surprise.

1151
00:51:51,681 --> 00:51:53,681
The surprise is you don't need

1152
00:51:53,681 --> 00:51:55,681
a million examples of something to learn.

1153
00:51:55,681 --> 00:51:57,681
You can do it with one example

1154
00:51:57,681 --> 00:51:59,681
if you're smart enough to

1155
00:51:59,681 --> 00:52:01,681
make use of that example appropriately.

1156
00:52:01,681 --> 00:52:03,681
So that was the surprise.

1157
00:52:03,681 --> 00:52:06,681
You can learn something definite from each example.

1158
00:52:06,681 --> 00:52:12,681
Next item was the salient idea.

1159
00:52:12,681 --> 00:52:16,681
Now when I say salient idea, I don't mean important.

1160
00:52:16,681 --> 00:52:20,681
What I mean is an idea that sticks out.

1161
00:52:20,681 --> 00:52:23,681
Some theses, funnily enough, have too many good ideas

1162
00:52:23,681 --> 00:52:25,181
and you don't know what it's

1163
00:52:25,181 --> 00:52:26,681
all about because which one is it?

1164
00:52:26,681 --> 00:52:28,681
So you need an idea that sticks out.

1165
00:52:28,681 --> 00:52:31,681
And the idea that stuck out here

1166
00:52:31,681 --> 00:52:38,681
was the notion of a near miss.

1167
00:52:38,681 --> 00:52:40,681
You see, this is not an

1168
00:52:40,681 --> 00:52:42,682
arch, but it doesn't miss by much.

1169
00:52:42,682 --> 00:52:45,682
So it's a near miss.

1170
00:52:45,682 --> 00:52:48,682
And finally, you need to tell the story.

1171
00:52:50,682 --> 00:52:53,182
Of how you did it,

1172
00:52:53,182 --> 00:52:55,682
how it works, why it's important.

1173
00:52:55,682 --> 00:52:58,682
So that's a bit on how

1174
00:52:58,682 --> 00:53:01,682
to not so much get famous,

1175
00:53:01,682 --> 00:53:05,682
but how to ensure that your work is recognized.

1176
00:53:05,682 --> 00:53:07,682
We're almost finished because now

1177
00:53:07,682 --> 00:53:09,682
we're down to this last item,

1178
00:53:09,682 --> 00:53:12,682
which is how to stop.

1179
00:53:12,682 --> 00:53:15,682
And when we come to that, there's a question of,

1180
00:53:15,682 --> 00:53:18,682
all right, well, what is the final slide?

1181
00:53:20,682 --> 00:53:23,682
And what are the final words?

1182
00:53:23,682 --> 00:53:29,182
So for the final slide, let

1183
00:53:29,182 --> 00:53:34,682
me give you some examples of possibilities.

1184
00:53:34,682 --> 00:53:38,682
How about this one?

1185
00:53:38,682 --> 00:53:49,682
Well, you might see that slide and think to yourself,

1186
00:53:49,682 --> 00:53:52,682
there are a thousand faculty at MIT.

1187
00:53:52,682 --> 00:53:55,682
Nice piece of work, but not so much,

1188
00:53:55,682 --> 00:53:57,682
but it's only a tiny piece of

1189
00:53:57,682 --> 00:53:59,682
work if you divide by a thousand.

1190
00:53:59,682 --> 00:54:02,182
So you show a whole gigantic list

1191
00:54:02,182 --> 00:54:04,682
of collaborators at the end of a talk.

1192
00:54:04,682 --> 00:54:06,682
It's a kind of letdown

1193
00:54:06,682 --> 00:54:08,682
because it suggests that nobody knows,

1194
00:54:08,682 --> 00:54:11,682
well, did you do anything significant?

1195
00:54:11,682 --> 00:54:14,682
Now, you've got to recognize your collaborators, right?

1196
00:54:14,682 --> 00:54:16,682
So where do you do that?

1197
00:54:16,682 --> 00:54:18,682
Not on the last slide.

1198
00:54:18,682 --> 00:54:19,682
On the first slide.

1199
00:54:19,682 --> 00:54:20,682
All this was on the first slide.

1200
00:54:20,682 --> 00:54:22,682
These are the collaborators.

1201
00:54:22,682 --> 00:54:23,682
So you don't want to put them at the end.

1202
00:54:23,682 --> 00:54:27,682
You don't want a slide like this.

1203
00:54:27,682 --> 00:54:30,682
How about this one?

1204
00:54:30,682 --> 00:54:33,682
This is the worst possible way to end a talk

1205
00:54:33,682 --> 00:54:38,682
because this slide can be up there for 20 minutes.

1206
00:54:38,682 --> 00:54:40,682
I've seen it happen.

1207
00:54:40,682 --> 00:54:43,682
It squanders real estate.

1208
00:54:43,682 --> 00:54:46,682
It squanders an opportunity to tell people who you are.

1209
00:54:46,682 --> 00:54:48,682
It's just...

1210
00:54:48,682 --> 00:54:50,682
What about this one?

1211
00:54:50,682 --> 00:54:52,682
I often see it.

1212
00:54:52,682 --> 00:54:55,682
I've never seen anybody write it down.

1213
00:54:55,682 --> 00:54:58,682
Also, it wastes opportunity.

1214
00:54:58,682 --> 00:55:00,682
Oh, my God.

1215
00:55:00,682 --> 00:55:02,682
Even worse.

1216
00:55:02,682 --> 00:55:05,682
All of these slides do nothing for you.

1217
00:55:05,682 --> 00:55:07,682
They waste your time.

1218
00:55:07,682 --> 00:55:09,682
They waste your time.

1219
00:55:09,682 --> 00:55:11,682
They waste your time.

1220
00:55:11,682 --> 00:55:13,682
They waste your time.

1221
00:55:13,682 --> 00:55:15,682
They waste your time.

1222
00:55:15,682 --> 00:55:17,682
They waste your time.

1223
00:55:17,682 --> 00:55:20,682
They waste an opportunity for you to tell people,

1224
00:55:20,682 --> 00:55:22,682
to leave people with what you...

1225
00:55:22,682 --> 00:55:24,682
with who you are.

1226
00:55:24,682 --> 00:55:27,682
Well, what about this?

1227
00:55:27,682 --> 00:55:30,682
This a good one?

1228
00:55:30,682 --> 00:55:32,682
It might seem so at first,

1229
00:55:32,682 --> 00:55:37,682
but here's the problem.

1230
00:55:37,682 --> 00:55:40,682
If you say these are my conclusions,

1231
00:55:40,682 --> 00:55:42,682
these are perfectly legitimate conclusions

1232
00:55:42,682 --> 00:55:44,682
that nobody cares about.

1233
00:55:44,682 --> 00:55:46,682
What they care about is what you have done.

1234
00:55:46,682 --> 00:55:49,682
And that's why your final slide should have this label.

1235
00:55:49,682 --> 00:55:52,682
Contributions.

1236
00:55:52,682 --> 00:55:54,682
It's a mirror of what I said over there

1237
00:55:54,682 --> 00:55:57,682
about how job talks ought to be like a sandwich.

1238
00:55:57,682 --> 00:55:59,682
And the final slide, the one that's up there

1239
00:55:59,682 --> 00:56:01,682
while people are asking questions and filing out,

1240
00:56:01,682 --> 00:56:03,182
it ought to be the one

1241
00:56:03,182 --> 00:56:04,682
that has your contributions on it.

1242
00:56:04,682 --> 00:56:08,682
Here's an example from my own stump speech.

1243
00:56:08,682 --> 00:56:12,682
Yeah, this is what I talk about a lot.

1244
00:56:12,682 --> 00:56:16,682
Yes, here are the things that I typically demonstrate.

1245
00:56:16,682 --> 00:56:22,682
And I wait for people to read it.

1246
00:56:22,682 --> 00:56:24,682
And then the final element there is

1247
00:56:24,682 --> 00:56:27,682
this is what we get out of it.

1248
00:56:27,682 --> 00:56:31,682
So that's an example of a contribution slide.

1249
00:56:31,682 --> 00:56:36,682
All right, now, what about the other part?

1250
00:56:36,682 --> 00:56:38,682
You know, you've got your final slide up there.

1251
00:56:38,682 --> 00:56:40,682
It's a contribution slide.

1252
00:56:40,682 --> 00:56:43,682
Somehow you have to tell people you're finished.

1253
00:56:43,682 --> 00:56:45,682
So let's check out a few possibilities.

1254
00:56:45,682 --> 00:56:52,682
One thing you could do in the final words

1255
00:56:52,682 --> 00:56:55,682
is you could tell a joke.

1256
00:56:55,682 --> 00:57:00,682
It's okay.

1257
00:57:00,682 --> 00:57:03,682
By the time you're done, people have adjusted themselves

1258
00:57:03,682 --> 00:57:04,682
to your voice parameters.

1259
00:57:04,682 --> 00:57:06,682
They're ready for a joke.

1260
00:57:06,682 --> 00:57:09,182
I was sitting in another

1261
00:57:09,182 --> 00:57:11,682
bar this time in Austin, Texas,

1262
00:57:11,682 --> 00:57:14,682
with a colleague of mine named Doug Lenat.

1263
00:57:14,682 --> 00:57:15,682
And he said,

1264
00:57:15,682 --> 00:57:18,682
Doug's a fantastic speaker.

1265
00:57:18,682 --> 00:57:20,682
And so I said to Doug,

1266
00:57:20,682 --> 00:57:22,682
Doug, you're a fantastic speaker.

1267
00:57:22,682 --> 00:57:24,682
What's your secret?

1268
00:57:24,682 --> 00:57:25,682
And he said,

1269
00:57:25,682 --> 00:57:28,682
Oh, I always finish with a joke,

1270
00:57:28,682 --> 00:57:30,182
and that way people think

1271
00:57:30,182 --> 00:57:31,682
they've had fun the whole time.

1272
00:57:34,682 --> 00:57:38,682
So, yeah, a joke will work down there.

1273
00:57:38,682 --> 00:57:40,682
How about this one?

1274
00:57:45,682 --> 00:57:51,682
Thank you.

1275
00:57:51,682 --> 00:57:54,682
I don't recommend it.

1276
00:57:54,682 --> 00:57:57,682
It's a weak move.

1277
00:57:57,682 --> 00:57:59,182
You will not go to

1278
00:57:59,182 --> 00:58:00,682
hell if you conclude your talk

1279
00:58:00,682 --> 00:58:02,682
by saying thank you, but it's a weak move.

1280
00:58:02,682 --> 00:58:04,682
And here's why.

1281
00:58:04,682 --> 00:58:06,682
When you say thank you, even worse,

1282
00:58:06,682 --> 00:58:08,682
thank you for listening,

1283
00:58:08,682 --> 00:58:10,682
it suggests that everybody has stayed that long

1284
00:58:10,682 --> 00:58:12,682
out of politeness,

1285
00:58:12,682 --> 00:58:13,682
and that they had a

1286
00:58:13,682 --> 00:58:14,682
profound desire to be somewhere else,

1287
00:58:14,682 --> 00:58:16,682
but they're so polite they stuck it out.

1288
00:58:16,682 --> 00:58:19,682
And that's what you're thanking them for.

1289
00:58:19,682 --> 00:58:21,682
So once wild applause has started,

1290
00:58:21,682 --> 00:58:22,682
you can mouth a thank you,

1291
00:58:22,682 --> 00:58:24,682
and there's nothing wrong with that.

1292
00:58:24,682 --> 00:58:27,182
But the last thing you do

1293
00:58:27,182 --> 00:58:29,682
should not be saying thank you.

1294
00:58:29,682 --> 00:58:30,682
Now you say to me,

1295
00:58:30,682 --> 00:58:32,682
Well, doesn't everybody say thank you?

1296
00:58:32,682 --> 00:58:34,682
Well, what everybody does is

1297
00:58:34,682 --> 00:58:36,682
not necessarily the right thing.

1298
00:58:36,682 --> 00:58:39,682
And I'd like to illustrate how some talks can end

1299
00:58:39,682 --> 00:58:41,682
without saying thank you.

1300
00:58:41,682 --> 00:58:43,682
I like to draw from political speeches,

1301
00:58:43,682 --> 00:58:46,682
but the ones that I've heard recently aren't so good.

1302
00:58:46,682 --> 00:58:50,182
So I'm going to have

1303
00:58:50,182 --> 00:58:53,682
to go back a little bit.

1304
00:58:53,682 --> 00:58:56,682
So here's Governor Christie.

1305
00:58:56,682 --> 00:59:02,682
He gave the Republican keynote address one year.

1306
00:59:02,682 --> 00:59:04,682
This is the end of his talk.

1307
00:59:04,682 --> 00:59:05,682
Let's see what he does.

1308
00:59:05,682 --> 00:59:10,682
And together, everybody, together,

1309
00:59:10,682 --> 00:59:12,682
we will stand up once again,

1310
00:59:12,682 --> 00:59:16,682
for American greatness for our children and grandchildren.

1311
00:59:16,682 --> 00:59:18,682
God bless you, and God bless America.

1312
00:59:21,682 --> 00:59:25,682
That's a classic benediction ending.

1313
00:59:25,682 --> 00:59:28,682
God bless you, God bless America.

1314
00:59:28,682 --> 00:59:30,682
Now, I don't want to be partisan about this,

1315
00:59:30,682 --> 00:59:33,682
so I think I'd better switch to the keynote address

1316
00:59:33,682 --> 00:59:36,682
and the Democratic Convention.

1317
00:59:36,682 --> 00:59:39,682
I was delivered that year by Bill Clinton,

1318
00:59:39,682 --> 00:59:41,682
who knows something about how to do politics.

1319
00:59:41,682 --> 00:59:43,682
Who knows something about how to speak.

1320
00:59:45,682 --> 00:59:48,682
If that is what you want,

1321
00:59:48,682 --> 00:59:50,682
if that is what you believe,

1322
00:59:50,682 --> 00:59:53,682
you must vote and you must re-elect

1323
00:59:53,682 --> 00:59:56,682
President Barack Obama.

1324
00:59:56,682 --> 00:59:59,682
God bless you, and God bless America.

1325
01:00:09,682 --> 01:00:10,682
Now, watch this.

1326
01:00:10,682 --> 01:00:12,682
Let's go back a little bit and redo it.

1327
01:00:12,682 --> 01:00:13,682
What I want you to

1328
01:00:13,682 --> 01:00:14,682
see is that at one point,

1329
01:00:14,682 --> 01:00:16,682
he seems to be almost pressing his lips together,

1330
01:00:16,682 --> 01:00:19,682
forcing himself not to say thank you.

1331
01:00:19,682 --> 01:00:21,682
Then there's another place where he does a little salute.

1332
01:00:21,682 --> 01:00:23,682
So watch for those this time around.

1333
01:00:23,682 --> 01:00:26,682
If that is what you want,

1334
01:00:26,682 --> 01:00:29,682
if that is what you believe,

1335
01:00:29,682 --> 01:00:32,682
you must vote and you must re-elect

1336
01:00:32,682 --> 01:00:35,682
President Barack Obama.

1337
01:00:35,682 --> 01:00:38,682
God bless you, and God bless America.

1338
01:00:38,682 --> 01:00:39,682
That's where his person is.

1339
01:00:39,682 --> 01:00:41,682
That's where he's pressing his lips.

1340
01:00:41,682 --> 01:00:43,682
There's a salute.

1341
01:00:49,682 --> 01:00:50,682
Yeah, I think that's pretty good.

1342
01:00:50,682 --> 01:00:52,682
Now, what are we going to take away from this?

1343
01:00:52,682 --> 01:00:56,682
Well, I suppose I could conclude this talk by saying,

1344
01:00:56,682 --> 01:00:58,682
God bless you, and God bless America.

1345
01:00:58,682 --> 01:01:01,682
Institute of Technology.

1346
01:01:01,682 --> 01:01:04,682
But it might not work so well.

1347
01:01:04,682 --> 01:01:06,182
But what you can get out of this

1348
01:01:06,182 --> 01:01:07,682
is you don't have to say thank you.

1349
01:01:07,682 --> 01:01:08,682
There are other things you can do.

1350
01:01:09,682 --> 01:01:11,682
And you know, it's interesting that over time,

1351
01:01:11,682 --> 01:01:12,682
people figure this out,

1352
01:01:12,682 --> 01:01:15,682
and there are some stock ways of ending things.

1353
01:01:15,682 --> 01:01:17,682
